[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 问题: Two quantum states with energies E1 and E2 have a lifetime of 10^-9 sec and 10^-8 sec, respectively. We want to clearly distinguish these two energy levels. Which one of the following options could be their energy difference so that they can be clearly resolved?
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] A. 10^-8 eV
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. 10^-4 eV
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C. 10^-9 eV
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] D. 10^-11 eV
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Please select the correct answer and provide the final option letter and its corresponding content.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] To distinguish two quantum states with energies \( E_1 \) and \( E_2 \), the energy difference must be larger than the uncertainty in their energies due to their finite lifetimes. The energy uncertainty \(\Delta E\) is related to the lifetime \(\tau\) by the energy-time uncertainty principle:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{\tau}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] where \(\hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi}\) and \( h \approx 4.1357 \times 10^{-15} \, \text{eV·s} \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 1: Calculate the energy uncertainty for each state.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] For state 1 with lifetime \(\tau_1 = 10^{-9}\) s:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \Delta E_1 = \frac{\hbar}{\tau_1} = \frac{4.1357 \times 10^{-15} / (2\pi)}{10^{-9}} = \frac{4.1357 \times 10^{-15}}{6.2832 \times 10^{-9}} \approx 6.58 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{eV}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] For state 2 with lifetime \(\tau_2 = 10^{-8}\) s:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \Delta E_2 = \frac{\hbar}{\tau_2} = \frac{4.1357 \times 10^{-15} / (2\pi)}{10^{-8}} = \frac{4.1357 \times 10^{-15}}{6.2832 \times 10^{-8}} \approx 6.58 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{eV}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 2: Required energy difference to resolve the two states.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] To distinguish the two energy levels, the energy difference \(\Delta E = |E_2 - E_1|\) should be greater than the uncertainty in the measurement, which is approximately the larger of the two uncertainties.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Here, the uncertainties are:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \Delta E_1 \approx 6.58 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{eV}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \Delta E_2 \approx 6.58 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{eV}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The larger is \(\Delta E_1 = 6.58 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{eV}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, the energy difference should be:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \Delta E > 6.58 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{eV}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 3: Compare this with the options.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] A. \(10^{-8} \, \text{eV}\) (which is \(1 \times 10^{-8}\) eV, smaller than \(6.58 \times 10^{-7}\), NO)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. \(10^{-4} \, \text{eV}\) (which is \(1 \times 10^{-4}\) eV, larger than \(6.58 \times 10^{-7}\), YES)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C. \(10^{-9} \, \text{eV}\) (which is \(1 \times 10^{-9}\) eV, smaller than \(6.58 \times 10^{-7}\), NO)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] D. \(10^{-11} \, \text{eV}\) (which is \(1 \times 10^{-11}\) eV, smaller than \(6.58 \times 10^{-7}\), NO)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Only option B has an energy difference large enough to clearly resolve the two energy levels.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Final answer:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] **B. \(10^{-4} \, \text{eV}\)**
