[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85]   - Water: 132°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85]   - Hexadecane: 102°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Rough surface contact angle for water: 148°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - We want: Contact angle of octane on the rough surface, assuming Cassie-Baxter state.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Options: 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85]   A. 134°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85]   B. 129°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85]   C. 139°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85]   D. 124°
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 1: Understand the Cassie-Baxter model
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The Cassie-Baxter equation for contact angles on a rough surface is:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \cos \theta^* = f_s \cos \theta + f_a \cos \theta_a
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Where:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(\theta^*\) is the apparent contact angle on rough surface
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(f_s\) is the fraction of solid surface area in contact with the liquid
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(f_a = 1 - f_s\) is the fraction of air pockets
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(\theta\) is the contact angle on a smooth surface (intrinsic contact angle)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(\theta_a = 180^\circ\) (contact angle of air, because air repels the liquid)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since \(\cos 180^\circ = -1\), we have:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \cos \theta^* = f_s \cos \theta - (1 - f_s)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] This can be rearranged to find \(f_s\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \cos \theta^* = f_s (\cos \theta + 1) - 1
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] f_s = \frac{\cos \theta^* + 1}{\cos \theta + 1}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 2: Calculate \(f_s\) from water data
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] For water:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(\theta_{\text{smooth}} = 132^\circ\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(\theta^*_{\text{rough}} = 148^\circ\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Calculate cosines (angles in degrees):
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \cos(132^\circ) = \cos(180^\circ - 48^\circ) = -\cos 48^\circ = -0.6691
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \cos(148^\circ) = \cos(180^\circ - 32^\circ) = -\cos 32^\circ = -0.8480
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Calculate \(f_s\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] f_s = \frac{\cos 148^\circ + 1}{\cos 132^\circ + 1} = \frac{-0.8480 + 1}{-0.6691 + 1} = \frac{0.152}{0.331} = 0.459
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] So the solid fraction \(f_s \approx 0.459\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 3: Use \(f_s\) for octane
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] We are asked to estimate the rough surface contact angle for octane.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] We know hexadecane contact angle on smooth surface is 102°, and water is 132°.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The octane contact angle on smooth surface is not given. However, octane is less polar than hexadecane (C16), so it is likely to have a contact angle close to but slightly less than hexadecane.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Let's assume octane smooth contact angle is similar to hexadecane or slightly less. Octane (C8) generally has a slightly lower surface tension than hexadecane (C16), so octane will spread more and have a smaller contact angle on most surfaces.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Assuming octane smooth contact angle is ~92°, but this is guesswork.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Wait, the problem is directly comparing water and hexadecane, but now asks for octane on rough surface.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] No direct octane smooth angle given, but std octane contact angle values on similar surfaces are usually around 90°-100°, closer to hexadecane.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] To solve precisely:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Instead of estimating octane smooth contact angle, we can assume the ratio of the contact angles for hexadecane and water on smooth surface applies similarly to octane values on rough surface.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Alternatively, maybe the problem expects us to use the hexadecane data to estimate solid fraction \(f_s\) and use it to estimate octane rough contact angle.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Likely the simplest assumption is that the roughness parameters are fixed and only the smooth angle changes for each liquid.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Water smooth contact angle \(\theta_w = 132^\circ\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Hexadecane smooth contact angle \(\theta_h = 102^\circ\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Rough surface water angle \(\theta_w^* = 148^\circ\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] From water data, we found \(f_s = 0.459\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Use this to calculate rough octane contact angle from smooth octane angle \(\theta_o\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] We don't have \(\theta_o\), but can estimate octane smooth contact angle from hexadecane 102° and hexadecane 107 mN/m surface tension while octane is about 21.6 mN/m vs hexadecane 27.5 mN/m.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Octane has lower surface tension, so the octane smooth contact angle would be less than hexadecane.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, assume octane smooth contact angle on the smooth coating = 90° (reasonable estimate between hexadecane and lower alkanes).
