[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 问题: We would like to dissolve (at 25°С) 0.1 g Fe(OH)3 in 100 cm3 total volume. What is the minimum volume (cm3) of a 0.1 M monobasic strong acid that is needed to prepare the solution and what is the pH of the resulting solution?
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] A. pH 3.16; 32.14 cm3
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. pH 2.04; 28.05 cm3
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C. pH 2.69; 30.09 cm3
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] D. pH 4.94; 20.40 cm3
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Please select the correct answer and provide the final option letter and its corresponding content.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Mass of Fe(OH)3 = 0.1 g
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Total volume = 100 cm³
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Acid concentration = 0.1 M (monobasic strong acid)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Temperature = 25°C
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] We are asked to find:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 1) The minimum volume of 0.1 M strong acid needed to dissolve 0.1 g Fe(OH)3.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 2) The pH of the resulting solution.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 1: Calculate moles of Fe(OH)3
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Molecular weight of Fe(OH)3:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Fe = 55.85 g/mol
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - O = 16 g/mol
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - H = 1 g/mol
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Fe(OH)3 = 55.85 + 3*(16 + 1) = 55.85 + 3*17 = 55.85 + 51 = 106.85 g/mol
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Moles of Fe(OH)3:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] n_{Fe(OH)_3} = \frac{0.1 \text{ g}}{106.85 \text{ g/mol}} \approx 9.36 \times 10^{-4} \text{ mol}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 2: Reaction of Fe(OH)3 with strong acid (H+)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Fe(OH)3 is a base and dissolves in acid by neutralizing OH- groups.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Reaction:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \text{Fe(OH)}_3 + 3H^+ \rightarrow Fe^{3+} + 3H_2O
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] It requires 3 mol of H+ for every 1 mole of Fe(OH)3.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Calculate moles of H+ needed:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] n_{H^+} = 3 \times n_{Fe(OH)_3} = 3 \times 9.36 \times 10^{-4} = 2.808 \times 10^{-3} \text{ mol}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 3: Calculate volume of 0.1 M acid required
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C = \frac{n}{V} \Rightarrow V = \frac{n}{C} = \frac{2.808 \times 10^{-3}}{0.1} = 0.02808 \text{ L} = 28.08 \text{ cm}^3
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] So minimum volume of 0.1 M acid to dissolve Fe(OH)3 is about 28.08 cm³.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 4: Total volume and H+ concentration after neutralization
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Total final volume = 100 cm³ = 0.1 L
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Volume of acid added = 28.08 cm³ = 0.02808 L
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Volume of water added = 100 - 28.08 = 71.92 cm³ = 0.07192 L
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 5: Determine pH of resulting solution
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Initial moles of H+ in acid added:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] n_{H+} = C \times V = 0.1 \times 0.02808 = 2.808 \times 10^{-3} \text{ mol}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Moles of H+ used to react with Fe(OH)3 are 2.808 × 10^{-3} mol exactly (Step 2).
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, all H+ has reacted, no excess acid remains.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] So, the solution should be neutral from the acid side.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] However, in a real scenario, Fe3+ ions hydrolyze and cause acidity.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Fe^{3+} ions undergo hydrolysis according to:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Fe^{3+} + H_2O \rightleftharpoons FeOH^{2+} + H^+
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] But this detailed hydrolysis is complex and not asked.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given that all acid is consumed, the pH will be governed by hydrolysis of Fe^{3+}, leading to acidic pH.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] It's typical that Fe^{3+} in solution makes pH below 3.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 6: Compare with given options
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Our calculated volume needed is about 28.08 cm³.
