[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Let's analyze the problem step-by-step:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] We have three genes: V, CV, and CT.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Mapping data given:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - V -> CV: 18.5%
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - V -> CT: 13.2%
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - CV -> CT: 6.4%
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The gene order on the map is given as:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] V - - CT - CV
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Meaning CT is between V and CV.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Now, Scientist 1 noticed that:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Sum of V -> CT + CV -> CT = 13.2% + 6.4% = 19.6%
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The distance V -> CV is 18.5%, which is less than 19.6%.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Why is the sum of the two smaller segments greater than the distance of the larger segment?
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] In theory, the distances between adjacent genes should add up to the total distance between the outermost genes, unless something happens.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The possible reasons can be:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] A. Erred loci placement: Maybe the gene order is incorrect.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. The gene order was reversed: Similar to A, incorrect gene order could cause the discrepancy.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C. A double crossover event: Double crossovers can cause underestimation of recombination frequency if not accounted properly.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] D. Recombinant interference: The phenomenon where one crossover affects the likelihood of another crossover close by; it usually results in fewer double crossovers than expected.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] But, the given data contradicts the expectation, because the sum of the two smaller distances is greater than the largest distance.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Normally:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Distance V -> CV ≈ Distance V -> CT + Distance CT -> CV
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Because the distance V -> CV covers the distance between V and CV, which includes both segments V->CT and CT->CV.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, if sum of parts > whole, this suggests the gene order is incorrect or loci have been mis-assigned.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] But look at the given order: V - CT - CV,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] If gene order were V - CV - CT (reversed), summing the distances differently may fix this.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Also, double crossover tends to cause underestimation of distances, but not an addition discrepancy like this.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Recombinant interference reduces the number of double crossovers, so interference usually reduces recombination frequency, but not cause sum of parts > whole.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, the most logical explanation is that the gene order was reversed.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] So the order is not V - CT - CV, but probably V - CV - CT, and that is why the sum of distances between V->CT and CV->CT is greater than V->CV.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Hence, the correct answer is:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. The gene order was reversed
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] # Final answer:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. The gene order was reversed
