[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 问题: In the last few decades, reverberation mapping, interferometry and various other observations across the electromagnetic spectrum, have been used to probe the inner structure of AGNs.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Various semi-empirical relations between the inner radius of the torus and the luminosity of AGNs have emerged. They can differ to an extent based on accuracy of observation and underlining assumptions.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] They are usually compered to the basic Barvainis model.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Assuming this model, and that the temperature of dust grain sublimation is 900 K, and bolometric luminosity of AGN is 1.5*10^46 erg/s; what is the inner radius of the torus?
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] (Assume bolometric correction of 5, for UV range luminosity)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] A. 0.6 pc
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. 0.1 pc
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C. 15 pc
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] D. 3 pc
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Please select the correct answer and provide the final option letter and its corresponding content.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] To find the inner radius of the torus according to the Barvainis model, we can use the following relation:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] R_{sub} = 1.3 \left(\frac{L_{UV,46}}{(T_{sub}/1500\text{ K})^{4.5}}\right)^{0.5} \text{ pc}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] where:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( R_{sub} \) is the sublimation radius (inner radius of the torus) in parsecs,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( L_{UV,46} \) is the ultraviolet (UV) luminosity in units of \(10^{46}\) erg/s,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( T_{sub} \) is the sublimation temperature of the dust grains in Kelvin.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Given:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Bolometric luminosity \( L_{bol} = 1.5 \times 10^{46} \) erg/s,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Bolometric correction for UV range = 5,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] L_{UV} = \frac{L_{bol}}{5} = \frac{1.5 \times 10^{46}}{5} = 3 \times 10^{45} \text{ erg/s}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( L_{UV,46} = \frac{3 \times 10^{45}}{10^{46}} = 0.3 \),
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Dust sublimation temperature \( T_{sub} = 900 \) K.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 1: Plug in the values into the equation
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] First, calculate the temperature term:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \left(\frac{T_{sub}}{1500 \text{ K}}\right)^{4.5} = \left(\frac{900}{1500}\right)^{4.5} = (0.6)^{4.5}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Calculate \( (0.6)^{4.5} \):
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] (0.6)^4 = 0.6^2 \times 0.6^2 = 0.36 \times 0.36 = 0.1296
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Then,
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] (0.6)^{4.5} = (0.6)^4 \times (0.6)^{0.5} = 0.1296 \times \sqrt{0.6} = 0.1296 \times 0.7746 \approx 0.1004
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 2: Calculate the term inside the square root
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \frac{L_{UV,46}}{(T_{sub}/1500)^{4.5}} = \frac{0.3}{0.1004} \approx 2.987
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 3: Calculate \(R_{sub}\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] R_{sub} = 1.3 \times \sqrt{2.987} = 1.3 \times 1.728 = 2.25 \text{ pc}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 4: Compare to options
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
