[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] A. 5.0x10^-3 M
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] B. 2.0x10^-2 M
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] C. 6.3x10^-7 M
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] D. 1.0x10^-2 M
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Please select the correct answer and provide the final option letter and its corresponding content.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Concentration of Ca-EDTA complex = 0.02 M
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Equilibrium constant \(K_{Ca-EDTA} = 5 \times 10^{10}\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Temperature = 25 °C (we can assume standard conditions)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - pH is ideal
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to find the concentration of free calcium ions \([Ca^{2+}]\) in the solution.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 1: Understanding the equilibrium**
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The equilibrium for the complex formation is:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Ca^{2+} + EDTA^{4-} \rightleftharpoons CaEDTA^{2-}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The equilibrium constant expression is:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] K_{Ca-EDTA} = \frac{[CaEDTA^{2-}]}{[Ca^{2+}][EDTA^{4-}]}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given that the complex concentration is 0.02 M, and assuming initially all the calcium and EDTA are in the complex form, we want to find the concentration of free \(Ca^{2+}\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Rearranged:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] [Ca^{2+}] = \frac{[CaEDTA^{2-}]}{K_{Ca-EDTA} \times [EDTA^{4-}]}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 2: Estimating the concentration of free EDTA**
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] In a stoichiometric 0.02 M Ca-EDTA complex, the initial concentrations of Ca and EDTA are both 0.02 M. Since this is mostly complexed, the free concentrations of \(Ca^{2+}\) and \(EDTA^{4-}\) are small.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Let the concentration of free \(Ca^{2+}\) be \(x\), and similarly for free EDTA \(x\) because the complex dissociates to equal moles of Ca and EDTA.
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] The concentration of complex is approximately:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] [CaEDTA^{2-}] \approx 0.02 - x \approx 0.02 \quad (x \ll 0.02)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Hence:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] K = \frac{0.02}{x \times x} = \frac{0.02}{x^2}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Solve for \(x\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] x^2 = \frac{0.02}{K} = \frac{0.02}{5 \times 10^{10}} = 4 \times 10^{-13}
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Taking square root:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] x = \sqrt{4 \times 10^{-13}} = 2 \times 10^{-7} M
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 3: Check the options**
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Our calculated free \(Ca^{2+}\) concentration is approximately \(2 \times 10^{-7} M\), which is closest to option C:
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] - C. \(6.3 \times 10^{-7} M\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:35:58 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given approximations, \(2 \times 10^{-7}\) and \(6.3 \times 10^{-7}\) are within the same order of magnitude. So option C is the closest.
