[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We need to find the degree of the field extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}, \sqrt{18})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 1: Understand the field
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}, \sqrt{18})
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Note that \(\sqrt{18} = \sqrt{9 \cdot 2} = 3\sqrt{2}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since \(3 \in \mathbb{Q}\), \(\sqrt{18} = 3\sqrt{2}\) is already in \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}, \sqrt{18}) = \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 2: Find the degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}) / \mathbb{Q}\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Both \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(\sqrt{3}\) are square roots of distinct square-free integers, so the extensions are independent.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] The degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})/\mathbb{Q}\) is 2.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Extending further to \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\), since \(\sqrt{3}\) is not in \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\), the extension degree is multiplied by 2 again:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}) : \mathbb{Q}] = [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}) : \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})] \cdot [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}) : \mathbb{Q}] = 2 \cdot 2 = 4
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 3: Conclusion
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since \(\sqrt{18}\) adds nothing new, the degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}, \sqrt{18})/\mathbb{Q}\) is 4.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Answer: B. 4
