[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Statement 1:**  
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] "If a group has an element of order 15 it must have at least 8 elements of order 15."
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 1: Understanding elements of order 15**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The order of an element \( a \) is the smallest positive integer \( n \) such that \( a^n = e \), where \( e \) is the identity.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - If \( a \) has order 15, then the cyclic subgroup generated by \( a \), denoted \( \langle a \rangle \), has exactly 15 elements.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The elements of order 15 in this subgroup are precisely the generators of the cyclic group \( \langle a \rangle \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The number of generators of a cyclic group of order \( n \) is given by Euler's totient function \( \varphi(n) \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] For \( n = 15 \):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \varphi(15) = \varphi(3 \times 5) = \varphi(3) \times \varphi(5) = 2 \times 4 = 8.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So, the subgroup generated by an element of order 15 contains exactly 8 elements of order 15.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ---
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
