[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Statement 1:** Every homomorphic image of a group \( G \) is isomorphic to a factor group of \( G \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - A homomorphism \( \varphi: G \to H \) has a kernel \( \ker \varphi \), which is a normal subgroup of \( G \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - By the First Isomorphism Theorem, the image of \( \varphi \) is isomorphic to \( G / \ker \varphi \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Therefore, **every homomorphic image of \( G \) is isomorphic to a factor group \( G/N \) for some normal subgroup \( N \).**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Hence, Statement 1 is **True**.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Statement 2:** The homomorphic images of a group \( G \) are the same (up to isomorphism) as the factor groups of \( G \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The factor groups of \( G \) are groups of the form \( G/N \) where \( N \) is a normal subgroup of \( G \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The homomorphic images of \( G \) are exactly those groups isomorphic to some \( G/\ker \varphi \) for homomorphisms \( \varphi \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - By the First Isomorphism Theorem, every factor group \( G/N \) is a homomorphic image of \( G \) via the quotient map.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Conversely, every homomorphic image of \( G \) is isomorphic to a factor group.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So, **homomorphic images and factor groups correspond exactly (up to isomorphism).**
