[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to find the degree of the field extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 1: Understand the field extension
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] The element \(\alpha = \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3}\) belongs to some extension field of \(\mathbb{Q}\). The field \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\) contains \(\alpha\), and the degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\) is known.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\) is 2.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Degree of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{3})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\) is 2.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Since \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(\sqrt{3}\) are independent algebraic elements over \(\mathbb{Q}\), \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\) is a degree 4 extension over \(\mathbb{Q}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}): \mathbb{Q}] = 4\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 2: Check degree of \(\alpha = \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3}\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since \(\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\), the extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)\) is a subfield of \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\), and so:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}): \mathbb{Q}] = 4 \geq [\mathbb{Q}(\alpha): \mathbb{Q}]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to find \([\mathbb{Q}(\alpha) : \mathbb{Q}]\).
