[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to factor the polynomial \( f(x) = x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + 1 \) over the field \(\mathbb{Z}_7\), i.e., modulo 7.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 1: Check which roots (if any) \( f(x) \) has in \(\mathbb{Z}_7\).**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Test \( x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Calculate \( f(x) \mod 7 \):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=0: 0 + 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 \neq 0 \)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=1: 1 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 6 \neq 0 \)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=2: 8 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 21 \equiv 0 \mod 7 \) (since \(21 \mod 7 = 0\))
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So, \(x=2\) is a root.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=3: 27 + 18 + 6 + 1 = 52 \equiv 3 \mod 7 \), not zero
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=4: 64 + 32 + 8 + 1 = 105 \equiv 0 \mod 7 \) (because \(105 = 7 \times 15\))
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So, \(x=4\) is another root.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=5: 125 + 50 + 10 + 1 = 186 \equiv 4 \mod 7\), not zero
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( x=6: 216 + 72 + 12 + 1 = 301 \equiv 0 \mod 7 \) (since \(7 \times 43 = 301\))
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So, \(x=6\) is another root.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, the roots are \(2, 4,\) and \(6\) in \(\mathbb{Z}_7\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 2: Write linear factors for these roots:**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] In \(\mathbb{Z}_7\),
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Root \(2\): \(x - 2\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Root \(4\): \(x - 4\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Root \(6\): \(x - 6\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since addition modulo 7 wraps around:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(x - 6 = x + 1\) mod 7 (because \(-6 \equiv 1 \mod 7\))
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So the factorization is:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
