[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Step 1: About a normal subgroup of order 7 in a group of order 42**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] By Sylow theorems:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Number of Sylow 7-subgroups, \( n_7 \), divides 6 (since 42/7=6) and \( n_7 \equiv 1 \pmod{7} \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The divisors of 6 are 1, 2, 3, 6.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Among these, which satisfy \( n_7 \equiv 1 \pmod{7} \)?
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]     - 1 ≡ 1 (mod 7) ✓
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]     - 2 ≡ 2 (mod 7) ✗
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]     - 3 ≡ 3 (mod 7) ✗
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]     - 6 ≡ 6 (mod 7) ✗
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So \( n_7 = 1 \).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Number of Sylow 7-subgroups is 1, so this subgroup is unique and hence normal.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore, every group of order 42 has a normal subgroup of order 7.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
