[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Polynomial coefficients: \(8, 6, -9, 24\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Leading coefficient \( a_3 = 8 \) is divisible by 2, but Eisenstein criterion requires \( p \nmid a_n \). Here, \( 2 \mid 8 \), so condition 2 fails.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Thus, \( p = 2 \) fails.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Check \( p = 3 \):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( a_3 = 8 \), 3 does **not** divide 8. Good.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( a_2 = 6 \), 3 divides 6.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( a_1 = -9 \), 3 divides -9.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \( a_0 = 24 \), 3 divides 24.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Check if \( p^2 = 9 \) divides \( a_0 = 24 \)? 9 does not divide 24 (since 24/9 is not integer).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] All conditions are satisfied with \( p=3 \).
