[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Let's analyze both statements one by one:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Statement 1: "If H is a subgroup of a group G and a belongs to G, then aH = Ha."
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - This statement says that for any element a in G and any subgroup H of G, the left coset aH equals the right coset Ha.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - This is **not necessarily true** in general for any subgroup H. Left cosets and right cosets can be different.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The equality aH = Ha holds if and only if H is a **normal subgroup** of G.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Therefore, Statement 1 is **False** in general.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Statement 2: "If H is normal of G and a belongs to G, then ah = ha for all h in H."
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - A normal subgroup H of G means that for all a in G, aH = Ha.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - But the definition of a normal subgroup does **not** imply that every element a commutes with every element h in H.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - That is, even if H is normal, it is not necessary that ah = ha for all h in H.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - For example, in the symmetric group S_3, the subgroup A_3 (the alternating group) is normal but not abelian, and elements of S_3 do not necessarily commute with elements of A_3.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Thus, Statement 2 is also **False**.
