[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given a group \((G, \cdot)\), the problem states that:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (ab)^{-1} = a^{-1}b^{-1}, \quad \text{for all } a, b \in G.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Recall the general rule in a group for the inverse of a product:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (ab)^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1}.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] This is always true in any group.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] However, the problem states that the inverse of the product is:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (ab)^{-1} = a^{-1}b^{-1},
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] which differs from the standard rule unless:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] a^{-1}b^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1} \quad \forall a, b \in G.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] So,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] a^{-1}b^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1} \implies a^{-1}b^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1}.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since \(a^{-1}\) and \(b^{-1}\) commute, and inverses are unique, this implies elements commute.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to show this leads to the group \(G\) being abelian (commutative).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Let's test: For all \(a, b\), we want to see if \(ab=ba\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] From \(a^{-1}b^{-1} = b^{-1}a^{-1}\), multiply both sides on the left by \(a\) and on the right by \(b\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] a(a^{-1}b^{-1})b = a(b^{-1}a^{-1})b,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] which simplifies as:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (ab)^{-1} = (ab)^{-1} \quad \text{(given)},
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] or equivalently,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] a a^{-1} b^{-1} b = a b^{-1} a^{-1} b,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] which becomes:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] e \cdot e = a b^{-1} a^{-1} b,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] so
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] e = a b^{-1} a^{-1} b.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Multiply both sides on the right by \(b^{-1}\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] e \cdot b^{-1} = a b^{-1} a^{-1} b b^{-1},
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] b^{-1} = a b^{-1} a^{-1} e = a b^{-1} a^{-1}.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Then,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] b^{-1} = a b^{-1} a^{-1} \implies a^{-1} b^{-1} = b^{-1} a^{-1}.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] This is consistent, but it doesn't directly lead to \(ab=ba\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] But inverses commute implies that the group is abelian as follows:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Consider the identity
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (ab)^{-1} = a^{-1} b^{-1}.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since generally,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (ab)^{-1} = b^{-1} a^{-1},
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] by equality,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] a^{-1} b^{-1} = b^{-1} a^{-1} \implies a^{-1} b^{-1} = b^{-1} a^{-1} \quad \forall a,b.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Then invert both sides:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (b^{-1} a^{-1})^{-1} = (a^{-1} b^{-1})^{-1}.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Recall that \((xy)^{-1} = y^{-1} x^{-1}\), so:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] (a b) = (b a),
