[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Given: (G, 0) is an abelian group.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Recall:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - An abelian group means the operation (denote it by "o") is commutative: g o h = h o g for all g, h in G.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The identity element is 0.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Every element g has an inverse g^{-1} such that g o g^{-1} = 0.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Now, let's check each option:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Option A: g = g^{-1} for every g in G**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - This means every element is its own inverse.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - This only happens in groups where every element has order 2.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Such groups are called elementary abelian 2-groups.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - But this is not necessarily true for all abelian groups.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Counterexample: The group of integers under addition (an abelian group) does not satisfy g = -g for every g.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So, A is *not necessarily* true.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Option B: g = g^2 for every g in G**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Interpreting g^2 as g o g:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - This means for every g: g = g o g
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Rearranged: g o g^{-1} = g
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - But g o g^{-1} is the identity 0.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So this says 0 = g for every g, which is not true.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So B is false.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Option C: (g o h)^2 = g^2 o h^2 for every g,h ∈ G**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Let's expand the left side:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   (g o h)^2 = (g o h) o (g o h) = g o h o g o h
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Since the group is abelian:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   g o h o g o h = g o g o h o h = g^2 o h^2
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So the equality holds in all abelian groups.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Hence, C is always true.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Option D: G is of finite order**
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The finiteness of G is not guaranteed by abelian property.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - There are infinite abelian groups (e.g., the integers under addition).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - So D is not necessarily true.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] **Conclusion**: Only option C is always true.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] # Final answer:
