[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to find the degree of the field extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 1: Consider the fields \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\) and \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{3})\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - The degree \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}):\mathbb{Q}] = 2\), since \(\sqrt{2}\) is algebraic of degree 2 over \(\mathbb{Q}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Similarly, \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{3}):\mathbb{Q}] = 2\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 2: Look at the extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since both \(\sqrt{2}\) and \(\sqrt{3}\) are square roots of distinct square-free integers, the fields \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\) and \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{3})\) are distinct quadratic extensions, and the union generates a bi-quadratic extension.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 3: The degree of the extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3})\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\) is equal to the degree of the compositum of the extensions.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Because \(\sqrt{3} \notin \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\), the degree is the product of degrees:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}): \mathbb{Q}] = [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}): \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})] \times [\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}): \mathbb{Q}]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Step 4: Determine \([\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}): \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})]\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - To find this, check if \(\sqrt{3}\) is in \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - If it were, then \(\sqrt{3} = a + b\sqrt{2}\) for \(a,b \in \mathbb{Q}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Squaring both sides would give \(3 = a^2 + 2b^2 + 2ab\sqrt{2}\), implying \(\sqrt{2}\) is rational, which is a contradiction.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Thus, \(\sqrt{3} \notin \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\), and the minimal polynomial of \(\sqrt{3}\) over \(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{2})\) is still \(x^2 - 3\), degree 2.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Therefore,
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
