[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to find the maximum possible order of an element in the group \(\mathbb{Z}_8 \times \mathbb{Z}_{10} \times \mathbb{Z}_{24}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 1: Understanding the problem
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] The group \(\mathbb{Z}_8 \times \mathbb{Z}_{10} \times \mathbb{Z}_{24}\) is the direct product of cyclic groups. An element of this group is a triple \((a,b,c)\) with \(a \in \mathbb{Z}_8\), \(b \in \mathbb{Z}_{10}\), and \(c \in \mathbb{Z}_{24}\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] The order of an element \((a,b,c)\) in the direct product is the least common multiple (LCM) of the orders of \(a, b,\) and \(c\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \[
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \operatorname{order}(a,b,c) = \mathrm{lcm}(\operatorname{order}(a), \operatorname{order}(b), \operatorname{order}(c))
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] \]
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 2: Possible orders in each cyclic group
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - In \(\mathbb{Z}_8\), possible orders of elements divide 8: 1, 2, 4, 8.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - In \(\mathbb{Z}_{10}\), possible orders of elements divide 10: 1, 2, 5, 10.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - In \(\mathbb{Z}_{24}\), possible orders of elements divide 24: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24.
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] ### Step 3: Finding the maximum possible order
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] We want to find \(\max \mathrm{lcm}(o_1, o_2, o_3)\) where
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(o_1 \mid 8\),
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(o_2 \mid 10\),
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - \(o_3 \mid 24\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Since \(o_1 \leq 8, o_2 \leq 10, o_3 \leq 24\), the maximum possible order is at most \(\mathrm{lcm}(8,10,24)\).
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] Let's compute \(\mathrm{lcm}(8, 10, 24)\):
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] - Prime factorize:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   - \(8 = 2^3\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   - \(10 = 2 \times 5\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   - \(24 = 2^3 \times 3\)
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85]   
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] The LCM takes the highest powers of each prime appearing:
[INFO] 10-07 19:43:56 run_experiments_1.py:85] 
